  "why": "The statement names Greenberg's conjectures on number fields: the invariants conjecture for totally real fields, plus the generalized pseudo-null conjecture and related claims. Results proving lambda = mu = 0 for infinite families of totally real fields (Qi-Stokes real quadratic, Chems-Eddin's biquadratic families, Chems-Eddin-El Mamry, Avila-Chnaras) restrict the universal statement to a class of fields, so they are cases rather than finite instances. Mercuri-Paoluzi-Schoof covers 30,394 explicitly listed fields, which is a finite check, so it is instances. Ichimura-Sumida, Mizusawa and the Barbulescu-Ray numerics are instances for the same reason. The elliptic-curve mu conjecture shares Greenberg's name but is a different conjecture, so I scored Chakravarthy and Lin-Yin as variant. This includes Lin-Yin, which Wikipedia's 2026 lead cites as resolving the invariants conjecture: the record shows the paper never addresses totally real fields or lambda, so the citation is a scope error. The CM-field and function-field analogues are variants too. Do, Li-Qiu and McCallum are reductions, or are conditional on unproven hypotheses (Vandiver's conjecture, pseudo-nullity conditions), so they are none. Lin-Guo gives at most t = 1 of the rationality conjecture, and the record says earlier results already supply that, so it is none. Chems-Eddin I is accepted in J. Number Theory, so it counts as digested. No AI system is credited anywhere.",
